ir small team going to Rune's homeland in search of help for Dansil which has been taken over by the Goddess' messenger (AKA the high sparrow). <br><br> <img src="https://images.gr-assets.com/hostedimages/1526564988ra/25556773.gif" class="gr-hostedUserImg" loading="lazy"><br><br><br>Cali and Rune struggle to decide whether being together is not only the right choice for themselves, but all the right choices for their peoples. The romance was subtle yet sweet and you craved the moments they weren't fighting or trying to "do the right thing."<br><br>Wil, while clearly scared, takes in stride his role and Dansil's new king. I liked that he didn't become high and mighty at the weight thrust upon his shoulders. He stayed the same silly Wil, just more jaded and serious in the face of politics.<br><br>Violet as usual was just annoying an realized she f'ed up big time. Though, blessedly, her chapters were reduced in book 2. <br><br><img src="https://images.gr-assets.com/hostedimages/1526565358ra/25556811.gif" class="gr-hostedUserImg" loading="lazy"><br><i> [Violet's face when she realizes she just a pawn and has f***ed up big time] </i><br><br>As for the plot and world building I'm sad to say it is largely undeveloped. What I expected to learn in book 2 about the world just never happened. The Eye (crystal) plot was poorly explained--and even after reading that part twice, I was still unsure of whether its role was good or evil. The magic structure with the use of crystals, whisperers, and mages was also not delved into nearly enough-in fact, barely at all. I don't think I fully understand their role.<br> <img src="https://images.gr-assets.com/hostedimages/1479753941ra/21231890.gif" class="gr-hostedUserImg" loading="lazy"><br><br> What does it mean for Cali to be the only other of her kind. Tell me more about the whisperers and their powers. What is the upkeep for keeping the crystal mines open and does Wil intend to re-cultivate the mining and go after Viva Sylthia for their cyrstals? What is the relationship between Wil's kingdom and Rune's kingdom at the end? <br><img src="https://images.gr-assets.com/hostedimages/1513074787ra/24660091.gif" class="gr-hostedUserImg" loading="lazy"><br><br><br><br>The book leaves far too many open ended questions at the end. Enough so that I was left deeply unsatisfied with the ending. Is it a decent ending? Yes, definitely. But, Lidell leaves many plots untied with crystals, viva sylthia, toxic air(?), whisperers, relationships, and more. I felt vastly disappointed getting an abrupt ending that felt like it was rushed. If this was to be so, new plot points should not have been introduced. The whole mines of Viva Slythia thing could have been taken out, as could the strife with Rune's kingdom, his daddy issues, etc. <br><br><br><br>I'd like to take a moment to say that despite all the negatives, it still gets 3 stars because I did quite enjoy the journey. I enjoyed the trek through the forest and the witty banter between characters. I only wish this had been a trilogy to give Lidell the time she needed to develop the world, magic systems, and characters that grew and developed very little. She is far more than capable as seen in her Tildor books as well as her Tides series, if just given the proper time.</span></div><div class=""></div></div></section></section><section class="ReviewCard__tags"><ul class="CollapsableList"><span tabindex="-1"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/3533544?shelf=arcs" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">arcs</span></a></span><span tabindex="-1"></span></ul></section><footer class="SocialFooter"><div data-testid="stats" class="SocialFooter__statsContainer"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium Button--subdued"><span class="Button__labelItem">1 like</span></button></div></div><div data-testid="actions"><div class="SocialFooter__action"><div class="Button__container"><